## {{ CLI_NAME }} (pattern engine)

This project uses the `{{ CLI_NAME }}` CLI to keep AI-generated content maintainable.
Patterns live in `{{ MAIN_FOLDER }}/{{ PATTERNS_FOLDER }}/<name>/`. Always prefer
patterns over one-off generation.

### Before writing any new feature or file

1. Run `{{ CLI_NAME }} pattern search -q "<what you're about to do>"`.
   If a pattern matches (score > 0), run it instead:
   `{{ CLI_NAME }} pattern run <name> --var key=value ...`
   Preview first with `--dry-run` / `-d`, then run for real.
2. Only write fresh content if no pattern matches.

### After generating new files

If you just generated new files and the work seems repeatable, ask the user
whether they'd like to capture it as a pattern before moving on. Only do this
for new file generation — not for one-off edits.

If the user confirms, capture it:
```
{{ CLI_NAME }} pattern gen -n <short-name> \
  -i "<intent keywords>" \
  -v "var1,var2" \
  -o '{"files":[{"name":"...","template":"out.njk","outputPath":"..."}]}'
```
Then fill in the template at `{{ MAIN_FOLDER }}/{{ PATTERNS_FOLDER }}/<name>/<template>`
(Nunjucks `{{ '{{var}}' }}` syntax, or a `.ts` file with a default-export function
`(vars) => string`). Validate before considering it done:
`{{ CLI_NAME }} pattern validate -n <name>`

### Pattern schema (`{{ MAIN_FOLDER }}/{{ PATTERNS_FOLDER }}/<name>/instructions.json`)

```json
{
  "name": "string",
  "variables": ["string"],
  "intent": ["string"],
  "output": {
    "files": [
      { "name": "string", "template": "string", "outputPath": "string" }
    ]
  }
}
```

### Quick reference

| Action | Command |
|--------|---------|
| Search patterns | `{{ CLI_NAME }} pattern search -q "..."` |
| Run a pattern | `{{ CLI_NAME }} pattern run <name> --var k=v [-d]` |
| Create a pattern | `{{ CLI_NAME }} pattern gen -n <name> -i "..." -v "..." -o '...'` |
| Validate patterns | `{{ CLI_NAME }} pattern validate [-n <name>]` |
| Usage metrics | `{{ CLI_NAME }} metrics summary` |
